#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133897 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133713 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "aa4400ff-09b0-596c-9857-c13881b72f73" :question/math-academy-id "e-9555" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C=C_1 \\cup C_2,$ where \n\n- $C_1$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_1(t) =-2 t^4 \\: \\mathbf{i} + t^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right],$ and\n\n- $C_2$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}_2(t) = -2\\: \\mathbf{i} -t \\: \\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in \\left[-1,1\\right].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region.\n\n![](images/14/147e8fdd97fc938ae5482b70d7215483956a860ee3ce69ae5bbebd111dcc343b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of $\\mathbf r_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf r_2(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-8{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540319 :question-generator/id #uuid "bd8e7e46-318a-5969-a3eb-109a69367586" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186133897.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f72d1468-b59f-55f3-9d92-ba00ddb0db88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132296} {:answer/id #uuid "fa256ecb-1155-587e-a827-4c7f7584f6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132297} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5293a6-7b3b-5ed3-a5f8-13eb0ad2c78d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132298} {:answer/id #uuid "5d10c8c0-0ad7-587d-9d4f-df30df144733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132299} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3d7569-0df8-52ec-b06f-90609c9c6e56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132300}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bb773cb5-403e-5d2c-95a1-5dbb8eba2e0d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132295} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8b5adde-1913-5b7b-8715-f70ae223e349" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370941} {:answer/id #uuid "5f438494-28b7-57fb-80d6-a3fe7e73a368" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370942} {:answer/id #uuid "e8b51f75-a136-57d4-bf07-dc90a22f9315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370943} {:answer/id #uuid "2260ecea-4586-5fe7-ac66-3cc46eb39c1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370944} {:answer/id #uuid "2f828455-578e-54d4-843d-b9476bb1f31f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8b51f75-a136-57d4-bf07-dc90a22f9315" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370943} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f079002-de78-5504-85d4-0c00298a5bfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c3df1fb-6ba7-5d92-9c02-3f4fe719a847" :question/math-academy-id "q-42215" :question/problem "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86f48c0b-2a3d-5163-b8e8-0c7233eb05fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132356} {:answer/id #uuid "e01d132b-6218-5a49-8b31-e23dbbbfbe93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132357} {:answer/id #uuid "ed54db10-308f-5357-92ca-c739526b9c08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132358} {:answer/id #uuid "fab5eb83-c4a0-5d86-82d7-ebea1c4f60b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132359} {:answer/id #uuid "6d37aff8-08ac-5983-b3ea-04ddf202a770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86f48c0b-2a3d-5163-b8e8-0c7233eb05fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132356} :answer-field/id #uuid "30db4754-f10f-5c4e-a207-166a2bda9bd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c299dba6-b9c4-559b-b05a-803d3465ef0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42298" :question/problem "![](images/01/01a5f06674cacca4764f438a8c06f020659f0177a0b74952f40bc9072627ec3f.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}\\,i-{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=4t\\,i-3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-{t}^{3})\\cdot 4t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "107fbbcc-1cef-591d-838c-89092ff4b58f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369401} {:answer/id #uuid "8c11bbb3-2d45-5311-90fd-c92caf9bea8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369402} {:answer/id #uuid "2983dbf6-4125-5413-bca4-9b9acc749a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369403} {:answer/id #uuid "319ba55a-cea6-54fc-8482-99d900f9e65a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369404} {:answer/id #uuid "20585aa7-a901-5327-aa20-d6a600d555d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c11bbb3-2d45-5311-90fd-c92caf9bea8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369402} :answer-field/id #uuid "a332c9c0-5c80-59f8-b499-e9724c2807a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c42efa1-14e8-5edb-ba70-51dc3c6ff4b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-126722" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a89a14bced6f6700614cfad216070154cae165785458f817ea06d266edfde4e.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e634686a-03c3-5649-bd9a-7735236d04f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369408} {:answer/id #uuid "4d8ff954-ed9f-55d7-a5de-4eb98b4a7700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369409} {:answer/id #uuid "44a799f1-979a-58d8-8972-839c9e22e67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369410} {:answer/id #uuid "a7508032-3abc-5fc9-a2ba-c0eca938cd0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369411} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9679c6-90fa-580d-a0cd-427d1ddfb956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44a799f1-979a-58d8-8972-839c9e22e67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369410} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b0054e-e505-5919-822e-5e1dd45e03f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e16fd42-acd5-59dc-b36e-e5d23e861950" :question/math-academy-id "q-126725" :question/problem "![](images/41/41dc9af596ca43b1d0d61bb4273be46d3a3d1adc62f99a70fe53c72d69667093.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d263f40-9c2f-5a37-bde8-f22ac722349c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369429} {:answer/id #uuid "d98084a7-8c18-5006-8eaf-808ce526a650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369430} {:answer/id #uuid "c270fb54-3408-58f5-94c5-0ccc6a0d2bb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369431} {:answer/id #uuid "89fea390-1808-5719-8411-656965ac4356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369432} {:answer/id #uuid "f90a6d10-d2f7-5146-b3e5-bd6446824220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f90a6d10-d2f7-5146-b3e5-bd6446824220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369433} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbaaffb7-8edd-5da1-89b3-b92a5ba8c4dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b773c29-9e6f-5096-b07b-de5aaca35eab" :question/math-academy-id "q-126818" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9eb614f4f6e00c5fa03759a61d69734aa1c6eaf26d98d3c78b96052ca16162d0.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-2{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-2\\,i-t\\,j$ for $t\\in [-3,3].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-4t\\,i+9{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{t}^{3}\\cdot (-4t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =12[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd2f859-8cbd-5fb2-a799-4047912f2b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369450} {:answer/id #uuid "c80ede24-c0d9-5ecf-be0b-3875c8087b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369451} {:answer/id #uuid "b67159a4-a2c9-5c6e-a51b-b9e8fec0e05d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369452} {:answer/id #uuid "6d280bef-8091-5d89-be52-181cdb57aaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369453} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c20b2-9d53-5d85-af6f-b027ed46706b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c80ede24-c0d9-5ecf-be0b-3875c8087b10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369451} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29d4169-0dcc-5881-9d8b-1107b9cc1f1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7834afec-6e2d-5917-b360-ab0e90141806" :question/math-academy-id "q-126858" :question/problem "![](images/52/52ec0dfc4590daf9306c2389bd321acbf80f263c799fa4ddd4e5b8f662e0a2b5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-t\\,i+1\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\,i+4{t}^{3}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431051 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ee80ff-c5ae-55ec-a96c-b506acfb7915" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431021} {:answer/id #uuid "4addbefd-201e-5755-8641-7e27605c3311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431023} {:answer/id #uuid "7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431027} {:answer/id #uuid "b4200cd7-229f-5b2c-80d8-6fbc6195eb39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431034} {:answer/id #uuid "dd351131-c322-54cb-8179-38615de2670f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431036}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a937f5d-4690-55ac-bcf9-5662d89ca2a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431027} :answer-field/id #uuid "771735d3-fa1a-5273-a935-b05b06edb2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "819104c2-11eb-5b49-9398-d7d5879660f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-126727" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b51e252935f7077f3285258845d8d1e4957b83894037f968ee306910aa88bad6.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b151d74-5c74-545d-8b07-dea1d8453657" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431016} {:answer/id #uuid "32eb8a32-3dbf-536c-8383-dfb162184c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431019} {:answer/id #uuid "419ff17a-c941-551d-ad1c-89eccf44a609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431020} {:answer/id #uuid "4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431024} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a3e7c7-ac4d-53f3-be35-8650eb6afd4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b345484-1be0-5dbb-8ec3-b21aa5962d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431024} :answer-field/id #uuid "64517546-ac9e-59a3-822e-98cda097a61b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd34f4a5-5a97-5165-88b5-594ad30569e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-126859" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ebabcf1e3c3c3d2f4850d425657e7314c0135c81a432bfbbc8ae31ee39cb85d.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\,i-2{t}^{4}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-t\\,i-2\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\,i-8{t}^{3}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079819d9-6be2-54b5-a40a-e93e9bbceca5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442226} {:answer/id #uuid "30812918-b09d-5eac-98bf-46dd91f25063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442229} {:answer/id #uuid "6baabb12-e16e-5f6d-acae-67851198dc97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442234} {:answer/id #uuid "9beda9f3-8b47-5843-a2c9-b0565509b47e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442237} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6e358f5-54ad-55b9-afe1-8f1e0f017b3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442238} :answer-field/id #uuid "589fe02c-92ec-567f-8354-84271d506420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442247}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55130439-a2e5-560a-bbed-4ce47334253d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126757" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef44f01fb462cdf608f66a60dd11b8df798603bd204dca9d579ed50b370a84e3.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =2({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fe86c01-fa33-54c0-919e-aaad045de4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511016} {:answer/id #uuid "91cc57eb-09e7-59eb-aa0f-280a8c492891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511083} {:answer/id #uuid "94960e82-187d-589c-a1c2-ed096c94c497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511087} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} {:answer/id #uuid "f46a71c6-0e54-5d62-9732-93eefd0150bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511288}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa8abc-d95e-51ca-a4d1-7a1aa516b455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511168} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdfd2c93-cc42-5e18-b039-6099f904aa29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "053eddb0-5c93-560e-b92c-a89951cbd49c" :question/math-academy-id "q-42239" :question/problem "![](images/93/938f91220033a2174c011c1f8d65ff61e81e79b8af0ef718411169364c885418.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=t\\,i+\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ \n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and   \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}t\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{t}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0]-[-\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3242ed2a-ee0f-5b6e-92a0-da63b077c141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510888} {:answer/id #uuid "482ab86f-4fe2-571e-b0ca-93e2bd5d4903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510943} {:answer/id #uuid "a3031b19-d41c-5adf-a1c1-fb61f1fe36c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511124} {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} {:answer/id #uuid "e19c04bb-3f86-514f-ad0d-22da5d1ba873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb43d5ac-3266-5668-89fa-191462493dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511167} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b42e264-51ff-5675-8ac9-a399601959a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad2198cd-84ab-52d9-a653-128de0924655" :question/math-academy-id "q-126754" :question/problem "![](images/86/86772e27f0a3226a608a1188fd8f58b9a8956f5f02fdbc5f9c2d29e2cef2a54c.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\text{d}u=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\cdot (-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡2t(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-18{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =18{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46da7648-8673-5c97-86f3-77238d0bb3c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 21 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540285} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6af0f6-c61b-5aba-ad4f-45f08e34969e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540287} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb604c3-2157-5d2e-9169-7495734d92f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540295} {:answer/id #uuid "f8052487-9788-50be-af33-0d64b549724a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540314} {:answer/id #uuid "fb479665-4249-5b02-8faf-a5ce6e2008fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb604c3-2157-5d2e-9169-7495734d92f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540295} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc5abd1-56ff-5eb8-8b28-6a4c03e533b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "001b4812-0777-5cfd-acd4-edb5e3c4b5a2" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=7\\cos t\\,i+3\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/88/88907a8e0d33f889ae4fe3723a239b904a302f41fa294fc372e9d73cb049128a.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -7\\sin t,3\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}3\\sin t\\cdot(-7\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=21\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(\\frac{21}{2}-\\frac{21}{2}\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[\\frac{21}{2}t-\\frac{21}{4}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(21 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=21 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=21 \\pi.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263ffce1-698e-508e-8873-25171957ad4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540279} {:answer/id #uuid "519536aa-01e2-580d-98bc-6634d5aa438d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540289} {:answer/id #uuid "7efe9a4b-a744-525f-9e01-9a860ae3c7e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540296} {:answer/id #uuid "87ae3b3a-947a-5634-ba17-7b4b33735361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540300} {:answer/id #uuid "b8967a6f-2070-58af-9e24-e6adc63c0557" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 30 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87ae3b3a-947a-5634-ba17-7b4b33735361" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540300} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9fb3ea4-caed-5a83-9c02-6440b3eab456" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4a32b1b6-1443-5fe5-830b-fde1a97630b5" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=5\\cos t\\,i+6\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/07/078f9509a08715c2a842bc9b8e12bfb3da9b9d77950275174ac684b399d06a68.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -5\\sin t,6\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\sin t\\cdot(-5\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=30\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(15-15\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[15t-\\frac{15}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(30 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=30 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=30 \\pi.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09abfe12-73cb-58a7-8f06-0ec5d416c06d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{42}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540278} {:answer/id #uuid "4290ff5e-39c2-5b7c-a87c-f3899caf9ee4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540283} {:answer/id #uuid "4f218fcf-9054-5c93-8b98-6c5f40e4a010" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540288} {:answer/id #uuid "79a46c85-2cce-5646-a4b6-2d01b6bc7a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{84}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540294} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf1fccd-682b-5c51-835c-1418e3129e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcf1fccd-682b-5c51-835c-1418e3129e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540318} :answer-field/id #uuid "d760dac3-e871-5a8d-a57b-1c489b8ed91b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "865c8611-6d60-511d-9bf4-cbb5278c6f73" :question/problem "![](images/07/078757ceefd30bb15326569c8fdd2f58b92a1a1cf9679c0d1ea662f387cbe553.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$, where\n\n- $C_{1}$ is parametrized by $r_{1}(t) = 7t^{2}i - 3t^{3}j$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1]$, and\n- $C_{2}$ is parametrized by $r_{2}(t) = 7i + 3tj$ for $t ∈ [- 1, 1]$.\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=14t\\,i-9{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 14t\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =42{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =42[\\frac{{t}^{5}}{5}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =42\\left(\\frac{1^{5}}{5}-\\frac{(-1)^{5}}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =42\\left(\\frac{1}{5}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =42\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{84}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540301 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64d60a05-7819-56b3-b386-06f85892d2ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 14 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540291} {:answer/id #uuid "6f31fa10-19e0-5042-88f2-07b6f7641a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540292} {:answer/id #uuid "74810cb1-4591-5367-94ef-00b68bd11c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540293} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ab43ba-bdf6-5064-8154-862a5df92d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540310} {:answer/id #uuid "e484ee97-fcad-5979-af5c-9c3886da5e78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f31fa10-19e0-5042-88f2-07b6f7641a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540292} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7b969cf-04ce-5446-b40f-f68d780677be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "927aab7c-38ca-5938-9f5e-f646a0bd4394" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=2\\cos t\\,i+7\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/92/92ce9f04a78465f03756605514aab3c48396c66eb8863f16a64b563a8cace6ee.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -2\\sin t,7\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}7\\sin t\\cdot(-2\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=14\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=14\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(7-7\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[7t-\\frac{7}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(14 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=14 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=14 \\pi.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540302 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2653811b-6a3f-50e5-80be-c5b24fcbaf1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540280} {:answer/id #uuid "368712d0-119f-5f7e-9dcf-f27b8a9ce142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540281} {:answer/id #uuid "3839d780-0e6c-5d0e-9f8e-06c50df07c5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540282} {:answer/id #uuid "4456e62e-40ab-5406-bdf9-5bc6653e3f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 48 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540284} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a69bfa-84cf-5837-871f-0d18bdd847e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5a69bfa-84cf-5837-871f-0d18bdd847e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540308} :answer-field/id #uuid "631a66dd-c989-5d40-b804-653902e5a82b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bd3c3fb-5e4d-5f92-a1a5-4e908f1d7704" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=6\\cos t\\,i+8\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/b3/b3fb73115ecc17ea35a3530602fe5851cba5a5a82c5bddb466d19fa82faf1d42.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -6\\sin t,8\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}8\\sin t\\cdot(-6\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=48\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=48\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(24-24\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[24t-12\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(48 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=48 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=48 \\pi.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf9b73fa-c7ce-5c93-9131-ec36d29d6c3a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86844522-ff99-5a89-bd9a-bb2ab32a4d4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540299} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5331c0-17c1-57d8-b981-80bc93013514" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540303} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b21cf5-b029-596f-9daa-1704136d990f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540307} {:answer/id #uuid "f49d5766-20e5-5f10-b5e7-ff819c91de23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540313} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d8532e-17d1-5493-9f25-92eb498314f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f49d5766-20e5-5f10-b5e7-ff819c91de23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540313} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6a49ae3-c511-509c-a7e2-5e82cb83cd8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5a8981d-48dd-5bc9-9468-7747b7d1e093" :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to find the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as $r(t)=3\\cos t\\,i+6\\sin t\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,2\\pi].$\n\n![](images/d0/d08df891c30fe6d4777fbbfd86887741d224beb3d71af7d20001521a417115a4.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=0-(-1)=1.$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D)&=\\iint_D\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,\\text{d}A\\\\&=\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\\oint_C P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y=-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x.$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$r'(t)=\\langle -3\\sin t,6\\cos t\\rangle.$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-\\oint_C y\\,\\text{d}x&=-\\int_0^{2\\pi}6\\sin t\\cdot(-3\\sin t)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=18\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\sin^2 t\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=18\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\frac{1-\\cos 2t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\left(9-9\\cos 2t\\right)\\,\\text{d}t\\\\&=\\left[9t-\\frac{9}{2}\\sin 2t\\right]_0^{2\\pi}\\\\&=\\left(18 \\pi-0\\right)-\\left(0-0\\right)\\\\&=18 \\pi\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{Area}(D)=18 \\pi.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Area Using Green's Theorem"}]}
